VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-014
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of intensely pruritic, weeping, and painful skin lesions disrupting sleep and functional ability.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage described a rapidly spreading, fiery red, and severely weeping skin eruption causing unrelenting itch and secondary pain. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out a disseminated secondary bacterial infection (e.g., Staphylococcal impetiginization) or eczema herpeticum, and to initiate immediate high-potency topical therapy and systemic symptom control to prevent further dermal breakdown prior to routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is completely on fire, I cannot stop scratching until I bleed, and the creases of my arms are weeping yellow fluid."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 72-hour history of an acute flare of their known atopic dermatitis. The exacerbation features intense, unremitting pruritus. The patient notes erythematous, ill-defined scaly plaques that have severely worsened in the dry winter months. The intense scratching has led to significant excoriation. Over the last 24 hours, the lesions in the antecubital and popliteal fossae have become extremely painful and have started to develop vesicles with a serous, crusting exudate. The patient has a strong personal history of atopy (asthma) and is currently unable to sleep due to the severe itch.
Functional Status: Independent, but severely fatigued due to sleep deprivation and distracted by constant pruritus.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of spreading secondary skin infection; significant psychosocial distress due to the visible and painful nature of the flare.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 120/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 37.3 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, anxious, and is actively, aggressively scratching their arms during the consultation.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals generalized severe xerosis (dry skin). There is a classic flexural distribution involving the antecubital and popliteal fossae, which display large, fiery erythematous ill-defined scaly plaques. Marked excoriation and chronic lichenification are present. Crucially, the flexural lesions demonstrate active weeping of serous exudate with localized honey-coloured crusting, indicating acute secondary bacterial colonization. There is no spreading cellulitic erythema, no systemic signs of sepsis, and no punched-out viral lesions suggestive of eczema herpeticum.
Environmental Observations: Multiple heavily fragranced soaps and a space heater are present in the room, actively exacerbating the extreme skin dryness.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been using a low-potency over-the-counter 1% hydrocortisone cream with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Exacerbation of Atopic Eczema with Secondary Impetiginization - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Eczema Herpeticum - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of classic punched-out, painful umbilicated viral vesicles and the lack of a high systemic fever.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the classic, symmetric flexural distribution and the strong personal history of atopy, rather than a localized reaction conforming to an exposure site.

Plaque Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the flexural rather than extensor distribution, the intense pruritus, and the ill-defined weeping plaques rather than well-demarcated silvery scales.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing an acute, severe flare of atopic eczema complicated by marked sleep deprivation, intense pruritus, and secondary bacterial colonization (impetiginization). The presence of weeping, crusted lesions and the total failure of over-the-counter management required immediate after-hours intervention. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in further aggressive excoriation, deepening of the secondary bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics, and ongoing severe psychosocial distress. Immediate physical examination allowed for the safe exclusion of viral complications and the prompt initiation of targeted oral antibiotics, potent topical corticosteroids, and sedating antihistamines to break the itch-scratch cycle overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Cephalexin 500mg PO QID for 5 days to treat the secondary bacterial colonization. Prescribed Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment (high-potency) to be applied strictly to active inflammatory plaques twice daily. Administered Promethazine 25mg PO stat to provide immediate sedation and break the acute pruritic cycle tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild the broken dermal barrier, apply a thick, unfragranced emollient ointment head-to-toe within 3 minutes of exiting a lukewarm shower, directly trapping the moisture in the skin before it evaporates. To stop unconscious nocturnal tissue damage, wear cotton gloves to bed, physically preventing sharp fingernails from excoriating the inflamed plaques while sleeping.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 to 5 days to ensure the bacterial crusting has resolved and to titrate down the topical corticosteroid to a maintenance regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a high fever, notices a rapidly spreading area of red, hot, swollen skin expanding beyond the eczema patches, or develops painful, fluid-filled blisters that look like cold sores, they must present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This is an acute flare requiring aggressive short-term management; long-term barrier repair is the cornerstone of eczema control.
Clinical Course: The oral antibiotic and strong steroid ointment will drastically reduce the weeping and redness over the next 48 to 72 hours, while the antihistamine will help you sleep tonight.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention immediately if your skin becomes extremely hot and swollen, if you develop a fever over 38.5 degrees, or if new, highly painful blisters appear over your rash.

